  longDescription:
    "IAIDO (居合道, literally 'WAY OF BEING IN HARMONY') is the JAPANESE MARTIAL ART OF THE DRAWN SWORD — the art of drawing the KATANA from its scabbard (SAYA), CUTTING one or more opponents in a fluid SINGLE MOTION called NUKITSUKE, performing follow-up cuts (KIRIOTOSHI, KESA-GIRI), removing blood from the blade (CHIBURI), and RE-SHEATHING the sword (NŌTŌ) — all performed as scripted solo forms called KATA. HISTORY: Origins traced to HAYASHIZAKI JINSUKE SHIGENOBU (1546-1621), a samurai from present-day Yamagata Prefecture who systematized the art of DRAW-AND-CUT (IAIJUTSU) in the late 16th century — the founding style is called MUSO SHINDEN-RYU (later evolved into MUSO JIKIDEN EISHIN-RYU). Multiple traditional schools (KORYU) developed over centuries: MUSO SHINDEN-RYU, MUSO JIKIDEN EISHIN-RYU, HOKI-RYU, TAMIYA-RYU, KATAYAMA-RYU, TOYAMA-RYU (military, developed WWII era). AFTER MEIJI RESTORATION (1868) samurai class was abolished + swords banned publicly; iaijutsu evolved into IAIDO — a spiritual + character-development discipline emphasizing INNER PEACE + AWARENESS rather than combat. UNIFICATION: In 1969, the ALL JAPAN KENDO FEDERATION (AJKF, 全日本剣道連盟) created the ZEN NIHON KENDO RENMEI SEITEI IAI — a set of 12 STANDARDIZED KATA drawn from major koryu schools — allowing iaido to be practiced + graded uniformly regardless of school affiliation. Most modern practitioners study BOTH Seitei Iai + one of the koryu schools. EQUIPMENT: (1) IAITO — BLUNT UNSHARPENED PRACTICE SWORD, aluminum-zinc alloy blade, same weight + shape + balance as a katana, used for safety in kata practice. (2) SHINKEN — LIVE-BLADE STEEL SWORD, used by advanced practitioners for TAMESHIGIRI (test cutting on rolled tatami targets) + advanced kata. (3) SAYA — the scabbard/sheath from which sword is drawn. (4) HAKAMA + KEIKOGI + OBI — traditional Japanese wide-legged trousers + jacket + belt. THE KATA STRUCTURE: Every iaido kata follows a similar structure: (1) SITTING or STANDING starting posture (seated katas practiced from SEIZA / TATE HIZA / STANDING); (2) NUKITSUKE — the initial DRAW-AND-CUT, ideally in one seamless motion catching an imagined opponent; (3) KIRIOTOSHI or KIRIKUDASHI — follow-up finishing cut; (4) CHIBURI — flicking simulated blood off the blade; (5) NŌTŌ — SLOWLY re-sheathing while maintaining ZANSHIN (continuing mental presence toward defeated foes). SEITEI IAI 12 KATA: (1) Mae, (2) Ushiro, (3) Ukenagashi, (4) Tsuka-ate, (5) Kesa-giri, (6) Morote-tsuki, (7) Sanpogiri, (8) Ganmen-ate, (9) Soete-tsuki, (10) Shihogiri, (11) Sougiri, (12) Nukiuchi. RANKS: KYU 6→1 → DAN 1→8 (like kendo). Master titles: RENSHI (~6 Dan), KYOSHI (~7 Dan), HANSHI (~8 Dan, highest). PHILOSOPHY: The KATANA cannot be drawn in anger; iaido teaches when NOT to draw the sword. 'The sword in the scabbard is the ideal state' — famous iaido maxim. TAMESHIGIRI: Advanced practitioners with shinken practice CUTTING TESTS on rolled soaked tatami mats — measuring blade angle, edge alignment, and cutting power. RELATIONSHIP TO KENDO: Sister art — most iaidoka also practice kendo (armored bamboo-sword sparring); AJKF governs both. GLOBAL: International Kendo Federation (FIK) governs iaido internationally; ALL EUROPEAN + AMERICAN kendo federations have iaido divisions; growing global following.",
